#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281374 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198401 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5a116e0d-3309-5148-a0ba-16993bc0deb7" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Flux as a Double Integral Given a Diagram"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d747dec-4db3-5966-9a2d-772570a4c2a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4{x}^{3}-2x{y}^{2}+6x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278717} {:answer/id #uuid "2b49f721-d34f-57b8-887a-a07af8acd027" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{x}^{3}-6x{y}^{2}-6x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278976} {:answer/id #uuid "3db364b4-51c0-5078-8edc-2aab3af9d03c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}^{3}+4x{y}^{2}-6x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279134} {:answer/id #uuid "6a21fb2b-62f4-563c-8f2f-7175d9195a03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{x}^{3}-4x{y}^{2}+10x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279552} {:answer/id #uuid "7a5aa06e-86ef-5b2a-8470-8113b5d11a67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}^{3}+2x{y}^{2}-10x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279682}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7a5aa06e-86ef-5b2a-8470-8113b5d11a67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}^{3}+2x{y}^{2}-10x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279682} :answer-field/id #uuid "99a942db-f0af-510b-bd6b-aeb36a731348" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281105}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fca4156b-f8d5-50fa-a7b9-3894a3eed862" :question/math-academy-id "q-161895" :question/problem "![](images/0e/0e374f18d8b87de2fddd9a2127ee84709746c7c1b75083441afcc7b6c79402fb.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=⟨-z,\\,0,\\,x⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that represents the boundary of the solid shown above. Let $D$ be the projection of $S$ onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,\\bbox[3pt,border: 1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AAAAAAA}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So, \n\n- the upper surface ${S}_{1}:z=g(x,y)=5-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ is oriented upward, and\n\n- the lower surface ${S}_{2}:z=h(x,y)=0$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper paraboloid ${S}_{1}.$\n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(5-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})=-2x \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(5-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})=-2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=-z={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-5,\\,Q=0,\\,R=x.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is upward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-5)\\cdot (-2x)-0\\cdot (-2y)+x\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2{x}^{3}+2x{y}^{2}-10x+x\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2{x}^{3}+2x{y}^{2}-9x\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the lower plane ${S}_{2}.$ \n\nSince the positive orientation is downward, the unit normal vector to the plane is $n=⟨0,0,-1⟩.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨-z,0,x⟩\\cdot ⟨0,0,-1⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-x\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux of $F$ across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2{x}^{3}+2x{y}^{2}-9x)\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{D}-x\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2{x}^{3}+2x{y}^{2}-10x\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405076 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198401 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5a116e0d-3309-5148-a0ba-16993bc0deb7" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Flux as a Double Integral Given a Diagram"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b802cb74-ee56-571a-ba2b-74472252f1f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "xy\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405078} {:answer/id #uuid "e6d7781c-10a9-5713-ab76-29cfaffd54d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-xy\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405079} {:answer/id #uuid "9a6025cd-45c8-596b-b893-c5fd2b0d6043" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405080} {:answer/id #uuid "f0f9eadd-2663-5edf-a933-30b97b13faa3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405081} {:answer/id #uuid "11453532-2079-5fc7-a118-668744b23d18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405082}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9a6025cd-45c8-596b-b893-c5fd2b0d6043" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405080} :answer-field/id #uuid "51599c73-a498-5c6e-99ec-3daf9c417dd7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405077}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1fddd3a8-32de-55ef-b058-e60e47b22c87" :question/math-academy-id "q-161897" :question/problem "![](images/6a/6af3d84594e2b2648dcbbae05a64f03ef5a38c76f5a1ed835fc4c6b6f50ef97a.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=⟨0,\\,-x,\\,-z⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that represents the boundary of the solid shown above. Let $D$ be the projection of $S$ onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-3\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich expression belongs in the blank when the upper and lower surfaces are parametrized as graphs over $D$ and their outward flux integrands are added pointwise where defined, before integration?" :question/worked-solution "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So, \n\n- the upper surface ${S}_{1}:z=g(x,y)=3-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ is oriented upward, and\n\n- the lower surface ${S}_{2}:z=h(x,y)=0$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper cone ${S}_{1}.$\n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}})=-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}} \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}})=-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=0,\\,Q=-x,\\,R=-z=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-3.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is upward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-0\\cdot (-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}})-(-x)\\cdot (-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}})+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-3\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-3\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the lower plane ${S}_{2}.$ \n\nSince the positive orientation is downward, the unit normal vector to the plane is $n=⟨0,0,-1⟩.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨0,-x,-0⟩\\cdot ⟨0,0,-1⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}0\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux of $F$ across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-3\\,\\text{d}A+0 \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-3\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198621 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198402 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d4d69d3c-3bb7-5e99-b606-1bbd538fa1c4" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Flux as a Double Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b0db8ab-29d7-5fe3-9f20-8a503e358b7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278839} {:answer/id #uuid "2b094c28-da56-5d15-a274-fa1ad40311ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y-x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278971} {:answer/id #uuid "706d0a96-7dfe-5cc7-b55c-f78d8b793955" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x-y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279612} {:answer/id #uuid "a892c1e1-5080-5e0f-8d79-ae3ae1774d7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2x-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280080} {:answer/id #uuid "fc1bdc04-3a79-54f8-8b7e-e69a71a66f75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280799}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1b0db8ab-29d7-5fe3-9f20-8a503e358b7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278839} :answer-field/id #uuid "b28d0bd3-3943-5d91-91b9-a9ce611eece5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281148}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5f651353-551c-55cb-9916-e19d1d757989" :question/math-academy-id "q-161907" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F=⟨1,\\,0,\\,-x⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that encloses the finite region between the paraboloid $z={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$ and the plane $z=1+x.$ Let $D$ be the projection of $S$ onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,\\bbox[3pt,border: 1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AAAAAAA}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So,\n\n- the upper surface ${S}_{1}:z=g(x,y)=1+x$ is oriented upward, and\n\n- the lower surface ${S}_{2}:z=h(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper plane ${S}_{1}.$\n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(1+x)=1 \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(1+x)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=1,\\,Q=0,\\,R=-x.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is upward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-1\\cdot 1-0\\cdot 0+(-x))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-1-x)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider lower paraboloid ${S}_{2}.$ \n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=h(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2x \\\\ \\frac{∂h}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=1,\\,Q=0,\\,R=-x.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is upward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & =-{∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}(-1\\cdot 2x-0\\cdot 2y+(-x))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}(-2x-x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3x\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-1-x)\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{D}3x\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2x-1\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405111 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198401 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5a116e0d-3309-5148-a0ba-16993bc0deb7" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Flux as a Double Integral Given a Diagram"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d997b13e-de60-5563-841f-2fd5ee29d99e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}+x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405113} {:answer/id #uuid "c47bf5a7-ddbb-55ea-94c5-903a6bd874ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}+y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405114} {:answer/id #uuid "a933c2f8-9508-5563-b677-d1cf1467dfc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405115} {:answer/id #uuid "9e7cafee-a688-51a7-b3d1-4d1daf466854" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405116} {:answer/id #uuid "156642d0-8eb1-5036-896e-6fea2101a8de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "xy\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405117}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9e7cafee-a688-51a7-b3d1-4d1daf466854" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405116} :answer-field/id #uuid "9854d104-9a4b-53d3-8285-a6f5f2377a4d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405112}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "883193fd-f5be-5d1c-8ffa-32b09d1431cb" :question/math-academy-id "q-161908" :question/problem "![](images/2f/2fff2eab1f5670dfcd3351dcbb9be1b3110e6ebdcd452cf4c5a273611d454c8b.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=⟨1,\\,0,\\,x⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that represents the boundary of the solid shown above. Let $D$ be the projection of the solid onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich expression belongs in the blank when the upper and lower surfaces are parametrized as graphs over $D$ and their outward flux integrands are added pointwise where defined, before integration?" :question/worked-solution "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So,\n\n- the upper surface ${S}_{1}:z=g(x,y)=0$ is oriented upward, and\n\n- the lower surface ${S}_{2}:z=h(x,y)=-\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper plane ${S}_{1}.$ \n\nSince the positive orientation is upward, the unit normal vector to the plane is $n=⟨0,0,1⟩.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨1,0,x⟩\\cdot ⟨0,0,1⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the lower hemisphere ${S}_{2}.$\n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=h(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}})=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}} \\\\ \\frac{∂h}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}})=\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=1,\\,Q=0,\\,R=x.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is downward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & =-{∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂h}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂h}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}(-1\\cdot \\frac{x}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}-0\\cdot \\frac{y}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}+x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}-x\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{D}\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}-x\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281379 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198401 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5a116e0d-3309-5148-a0ba-16993bc0deb7" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Flux as a Double Integral Given a Diagram"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "34a8ff49-f833-5f3c-850a-8d217758437e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}^{3}+2{y}^{3}-12xy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279047} {:answer/id #uuid "63e9c76d-0951-5e6a-9281-3e125cd1d310" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}^{2}{y}^{2}-2x+6y-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279500} {:answer/id #uuid "9aac3735-e148-5a13-a799-508ff10d7ca3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}^{3}+2x{y}^{2}-6x+2y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279963} {:answer/id #uuid "b48c9db8-b76f-57f1-9490-1c2b1d9bda32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x{y}^{2}+2{y}^{3}+2x-6y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280183} {:answer/id #uuid "e8f78e2b-f03d-51a5-a61b-fc3d2913a4bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{x}^{2}{y}^{2}-2xy-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280641}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9aac3735-e148-5a13-a799-508ff10d7ca3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}^{3}+2x{y}^{2}-6x+2y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279963} :answer-field/id #uuid "61b875c5-3db3-5698-aae1-ec503104d82f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281003}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2fe585d2-13a2-5e4d-ba70-80b8560f5587" :question/math-academy-id "q-161911" :question/problem "![](images/57/5709af87720dcb052d8ffc6cc600c5889e45849aee6bed69ec3646e43c3cefb1.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=⟨z,\\,1,\\,0⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that represents the boundary of the solid shown above. Let $D$ be the projection of the solid onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,\\bbox[3pt,border: 1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AAAAAAA}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So,\n\n- the upper surface ${S}_{1}:z=g(x,y)=0$ is oriented upward, and\n\n- the lower surface ${S}_{2}:z=h(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-3$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper plane ${S}_{1}.$ \n\nSince the positive orientation is upward, the unit normal vector to the plane is $n=⟨0,0,1⟩.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨z,1,0⟩\\cdot ⟨0,0,1⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}0\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the lower paraboloid ${S}_{2}.$\n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=h(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-3)=2x \\\\ \\frac{∂h}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-3)=2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=z={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-3,\\,Q=1,\\,R=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is downward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & =-{∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂h}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂h}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}(-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-3)\\cdot 2x-1\\cdot 2y+0)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}-2{x}^{3}-2x{y}^{2}+6x-2y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2{x}^{3}+2x{y}^{2}-6x+2y\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & =0+{∬}_{D}2{x}^{3}+2x{y}^{2}-6x+2y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2{x}^{3}+2x{y}^{2}-6x+2y\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
